WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.995
At first every day was the same for Mary.

2
00:00:05.996 --> 00:00:11.288
Every morning she awoke and found Martha kneeling upon the hearth building her fire.

3
00:00:11.289 --> 00:00:15.729
After breakfast she gazed out the window at the huge moor.

4
00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:22.096
And every day she realized that if she did not go outside, she would have to stay in and do nothing.

5
00:00:22.097 --> 00:00:25.115
So she went outside.

6
00:00:25.116 --> 00:00:28.442
Mary ran only to make herself warm.

7
00:00:28.443 --> 00:00:34.344
But the big breaths of fresh air filled her lungs with something that was good for her whole body.

8
00:00:34.345 --> 00:00:38.713
The air brought color into her cheeks and brightened her eyes.

9
00:00:38.714 --> 00:00:44.999
After a few days, she began to wake up hungry and eat her porridge.

10
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:53.336
When she went outside, there was one place she went to most often: the long path outside the walled gardens.

11
00:00:53.337 --> 00:00:58.146
One day she noticed that the ivy grew more thickly on one part of the wall.

12
00:00:58.147 --> 00:01:02.734
She was just wondering why this was so, when she heard a chirp.

13
00:01:02.735 --> 00:01:07.939
She looked up and saw Ben Weatherstaff's robin perched on top of the wall.

14
00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.291
"Oh!" she cried. "Is it you?"

15
00:01:11.292 --> 00:01:14.926
She spoke to him as if she expected him to answer.

16
00:01:14.927 --> 00:01:19.309
And he did answer. He chirped and hopped along the wall.

17
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:23.923
He might as well have been saying, "Good morning! Isn't the sun nice?

18
00:01:23.924 --> 00:01:26.320
Let's chirp and hop together."

19
00:01:26.321 --> 00:01:28.033
Mary began to laugh.

20
00:01:28.034 --> 00:01:32.961
As the bird hopped and took little flights along the wall, she ran after him.

21
00:01:32.962 --> 00:01:38.487
"I like you! I like you!" she cried as she ran down the path.

22
00:01:38.488 --> 00:01:42.594
The robin spread his wings and flew to the top of a tree.

23
00:01:42.595 --> 00:01:46.775
This reminded Mary of the first time she saw him.

24
00:01:46.776 --> 00:01:51.016
She had been standing in the orchard, and he had been at the top of a tree.

25
00:01:51.017 --> 00:01:55.620
Suddenly she realized that she was standing outside the orchard.

26
00:01:55.621 --> 00:01:58.831
"He's in the garden without a door," Mary said.

27
00:01:58.832 --> 00:02:02.818
"Oh, I wish I could see that garden!"

28
00:02:02.819 --> 00:02:07.721
She ran up the path to the green door that led into the kitchen gardens.

29
00:02:07.722 --> 00:02:11.853
Then she ran through the walled gardens and into the orchard.

30
00:02:11.854 --> 00:02:16.950
When she stopped and looked up, there was the tree on the other side of the wall.

31
00:02:16.951 --> 00:02:20.292
The robin was just finishing his song.

32
00:02:20.293 --> 00:02:26.170
"That's the secret garden," she said. "I'm sure it is."

33
00:02:26.171 --> 00:02:29.764
She looked closely but could not find a door.

34
00:02:29.765 --> 00:02:35.817
Then she ran through the kitchen gardens again and out onto the path outside the wall.

35
00:02:35.818 --> 00:02:41.543
She studied the long, ivy-covered wall from end to end.

36
00:02:41.544 --> 00:02:47.149
"It's very strange," she said. "Ben Weatherstaff said there was no door.

37
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:51.710
But there must have been one because Mr. Craven buried the key."

38
00:02:51.711 --> 00:02:55.105
Mary was quite interested in this mystery.

39
00:02:55.106 --> 00:03:03.187
She stayed outdoors nearly all day, and when she sat down to her supper that night, she felt hungry and sleepy.

40
00:03:03.188 --> 00:03:07.200
After supper she sat with Martha in front of the fire.

41
00:03:07.201 --> 00:03:11.852
"Why did Mr. Craven hate the garden?" asked Mary.

42
00:03:11.853 --> 00:03:16.989
"Are you still thinking about that garden?" Martha asked in surprise.

43
00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:21.187
"Yes, and I want to know why Mr. Craven hated it."

44
00:03:21.188 --> 00:03:23.495
"Oh, listen to the wind!

45
00:03:23.496 --> 00:03:27.153
You could barely stand up on the moor if you went out tonight."

46
00:03:27.154 --> 00:03:32.981
"Yes, I hear it. Answer my question. Why did he hate the garden?"

47
00:03:32.982 --> 00:03:35.057
"All right, I'll tell you.

48
00:03:35.058 --> 00:03:39.209
But Mrs. Medlock says we're not supposed to talk about this."

49
00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:43.434
Martha shifted on the hearth rug and began her story.

50
00:03:43.435 --> 00:03:48.019
"It was Mrs. Craven's garden, and she loved it.

51
00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:51.730
She used to tend the flowers with Mr. Craven.

52
00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:56.133
There was an old tree with a branch built like a seat.

53
00:03:56.134 --> 00:04:00.800
And she made roses grow over it, and she used to sit there.

54
00:04:00.801 --> 00:04:05.770
But one day the branch broke, and she fell to the ground.

55
00:04:05.771 --> 00:04:10.056
She was hurt so badly that she died the next day.

56
00:04:10.057 --> 00:04:15.141
The doctors thought Mr. Craven would go out of his mind with grief and die too.

57
00:04:15.142 --> 00:04:19.040
No one has gone into that garden since."

58
00:04:19.041 --> 00:04:22.070
Mary felt sorry for Mr. Craven.

59
00:04:22.071 --> 00:04:29.027
She didn't ask any more questions, but sat staring at the fire and listening to the wind.

60
00:04:29.028 --> 00:04:34.504
Suddenly she heard a strange sound almost as if a child were crying.

61
00:04:34.505 --> 00:04:37.428
"Do you hear someone crying?"

62
00:04:37.429 --> 00:04:42.108
"No." Martha looked upset. "It's the wind.

63
00:04:42.109 --> 00:04:46.474
Sometimes it sounds as if someone is lost on the moor and wailing."

64
00:04:46.475 --> 00:04:53.751
At that very moment, a gust of wind came rushing down the corridor and blew open their door with a crash.

65
00:04:53.752 --> 00:04:58.388
Now the mysterious sound was louder than ever.

66
00:04:58.389 --> 00:05:02.343
"Someone is crying!" Mary insisted.

67
00:05:02.344 --> 00:05:06.713
"Oh no. It's the wind," Martha said stubbornly.

68
00:05:06.714 --> 00:05:11.154
"Or it's the kitchen maid. She's had a toothache all day."

69
00:05:11.155 --> 00:05:14.493
Something in Martha's manner was strange.

70
00:05:14.494 --> 00:05:24.227
Mary kept quiet, but she did not believe that Martha was telling the truth.

